<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Proof of the Exact Cesàro Matrix Power Entry</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem.</span>
Let <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> be the lower–triangular Cesàro matrix</p>
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i,j}\;=\;\begin{cases}\dfrac{1}{i},&amp;1\leq j\leq i\leq L,\\[4.0pt]
0,&amp;1\leq i&lt;j\leq L.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i,j}\;=\;\begin{cases}\dfrac{1}{i},&amp;1\leq j\leq i\leq L,\\[4.0pt]
0,&amp;1\leq i&lt;j\leq L.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Then for any <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A1.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{H})_{i,j}=\begin{cases}\binom{i-1}{j-1}\sum_{m=j}^{i}(-1)^{\,m-j}\binom{i-j}{m-j}\left(\frac{1}{m}\right)^{H},&amp;1\leq j\leq i\leq L,\\[8.0pt]
0,&amp;1\leq i&lt;j\leq L.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>H</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{H})_{i,j}=\begin{cases}\binom{i-1}{j-1}\sum_{m=j}^{i}(-1)^{\,m-j}\binom{i-j}{m-j}\left(\frac{1}{m}\right)^{H},&amp;1\leq j\leq i\leq L,\\[8.0pt]
0,&amp;1\leq i&lt;j\leq L.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">In particular, taking <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=L</annotation></semantics></math> yields (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 The Discrete Causal Model (Cesàro Matrices) ‣ 3 The Exact Calculus of Position Bias ‣ Lost in the Middle at Birth: An Exact Theory of Transformer Position Bias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) in the main text.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
The upper-triangular case <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i&lt;j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i&lt;j)</annotation></semantics></math> is immediate: since <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly lower-triangular, <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly lower-triangular for all <math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the lower-triangular case <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j\leq i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j\leq i)</annotation></semantics></math>, we proceed by induction on <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Base case (<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=1</annotation></semantics></math>).</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leq i</annotation></semantics></math>, the right-hand side becomes</p>
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{i-1}{j-1}\sum_{m=j}^{i}(-1)^{\,m-j}\binom{i-j}{m-j}\frac{1}{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{i-1}{j-1}\sum_{m=j}^{i}(-1)^{\,m-j}\binom{i-j}{m-j}\frac{1}{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">With the change of variables <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=m-j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=m-j</annotation></semantics></math> this is</p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{i-1}{j-1}\sum_{k=0}^{i-j}(-1)^{k}\binom{i-j}{k}\frac{1}{k+j}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{i-1}{j-1}\sum_{k=0}^{i-j}(-1)^{k}\binom{i-j}{k}\frac{1}{k+j}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Writing <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{k+j}=\int_{0}^{1}x^{k+j-1}dx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{k+j}=\int_{0}^{1}x^{k+j-1}dx</annotation></semantics></math>, the inner sum is the integral representation of the Beta function</p>
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k=0}^{i-j}(-1)^{k}\binom{i-j}{k}\frac{1}{k+j}=B\bigl(j,\,i-j+1\bigr)=\frac{1}{i\binom{i-1}{j-1}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k=0}^{i-j}(-1)^{k}\binom{i-j}{k}\frac{1}{k+j}=B\bigl(j,\,i-j+1\bigr)=\frac{1}{i\binom{i-1}{j-1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Multiplying by <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{i-1}{j-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{i-1}{j-1}</annotation></semantics></math> gives</p>
<table id="A1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{1})_{i,j}=\begin{cases}\dfrac{1}{i},&amp;1\leq j\leq i\leq L,\\[4.0pt]
0,&amp;1\leq i&lt;j\leq L.\end{cases}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{1})_{i,j}=\begin{cases}\dfrac{1}{i},&amp;1\leq j\leq i\leq L,\\[4.0pt]
0,&amp;1\leq i&lt;j\leq L.\end{cases},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">which is exactly <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> by definition. The base case holds.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Inductive step.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume (<a href="#A1.E9" title="In Appendix A Proof of the Exact Cesàro Matrix Power Entry ‣ Lost in the Middle at Birth: An Exact Theory of Transformer Position Bias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) holds for some <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then for <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leq i</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{H+1})_{i,j}=\sum_{k=j}^{i}M_{i,k}(M^{H})_{k,j}=\frac{1}{i}\sum_{k=j}^{i}(M^{H})_{k,j}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{H+1})_{i,j}=\sum_{k=j}^{i}M_{i,k}(M^{H})_{k,j}=\frac{1}{i}\sum_{k=j}^{i}(M^{H})_{k,j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">since <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i,k}=1/i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i,k}=1/i</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leq k\leq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leq k\leq i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Substituting the inductive hypothesis for <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{H})_{k,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{H})_{k,j}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{H+1})_{i,j}=\frac{1}{i}\sum_{k=j}^{i}\left[\binom{k-1}{j-1}\sum_{m=j}^{k}(-1)^{\,m-j}\binom{k-j}{m-j}\left(\frac{1}{m}\right)^{H}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>H</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{H+1})_{i,j}=\frac{1}{i}\sum_{k=j}^{i}\left[\binom{k-1}{j-1}\sum_{m=j}^{k}(-1)^{\,m-j}\binom{k-j}{m-j}\left(\frac{1}{m}\right)^{H}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We now swap the order of summation over the region <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leq m\leq k\leq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leq m\leq k\leq i</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{H+1})_{i,j}=\frac{1}{i}\sum_{m=j}^{i}(-1)^{\,m-j}\left(\frac{1}{m}\right)^{H}\left[\sum_{k=m}^{i}\binom{k-1}{j-1}\binom{k-j}{m-j}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>H</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{H+1})_{i,j}=\frac{1}{i}\sum_{m=j}^{i}(-1)^{\,m-j}\left(\frac{1}{m}\right)^{H}\left[\sum_{k=m}^{i}\binom{k-1}{j-1}\binom{k-j}{m-j}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The inner sum simplifies via the binomial identity</p>
<table id="A1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{k-1}{j-1}\binom{k-j}{m-j}=\binom{m-1}{j-1}\binom{k-1}{m-1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{k-1}{j-1}\binom{k-j}{m-j}=\binom{m-1}{j-1}\binom{k-1}{m-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">so that</p>
<table id="A1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k=m}^{i}\binom{k-1}{j-1}\binom{k-j}{m-j}=\binom{m-1}{j-1}\sum_{k=m}^{i}\binom{k-1}{m-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k=m}^{i}\binom{k-1}{j-1}\binom{k-j}{m-j}=\binom{m-1}{j-1}\sum_{k=m}^{i}\binom{k-1}{m-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Applying the Hockey-Stick identity
<math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k=m}^{i}\binom{k-1}{m-1}=\binom{i}{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em">(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k=m}^{i}\binom{k-1}{m-1}=\binom{i}{m}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we obtain</p>
<table id="A1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k=m}^{i}\binom{k-1}{j-1}\binom{k-j}{m-j}=\binom{m-1}{j-1}\binom{i}{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k=m}^{i}\binom{k-1}{j-1}\binom{k-j}{m-j}=\binom{m-1}{j-1}\binom{i}{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Substituting back,</p>
<table id="A1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{H+1})_{i,j}=\frac{1}{i}\sum_{m=j}^{i}(-1)^{\,m-j}\left(\frac{1}{m}\right)^{H}\binom{m-1}{j-1}\binom{i}{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>H</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{H+1})_{i,j}=\frac{1}{i}\sum_{m=j}^{i}(-1)^{\,m-j}\left(\frac{1}{m}\right)^{H}\binom{m-1}{j-1}\binom{i}{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">A straightforward factorial simplification yields</p>
<table id="A1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{i}\binom{i}{m}\binom{m-1}{j-1}=\frac{1}{m}\binom{i-1}{j-1}\binom{i-j}{m-j}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{i}\binom{i}{m}\binom{m-1}{j-1}=\frac{1}{m}\binom{i-1}{j-1}\binom{i-j}{m-j}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Thus,</p>
<table id="A1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{H+1})_{i,j}=\begin{cases}\binom{i-1}{j-1}\sum_{m=j}^{i}(-1)^{\,m-j}\binom{i-j}{m-j}\left(\frac{1}{m}\right)^{H+1},&amp;1\leq j\leq i\leq L,\\[4.0pt]
0,&amp;1\leq i&lt;j\leq L.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{H+1})_{i,j}=\begin{cases}\binom{i-1}{j-1}\sum_{m=j}^{i}(-1)^{\,m-j}\binom{i-j}{m-j}\left(\frac{1}{m}\right)^{H+1},&amp;1\leq j\leq i\leq L,\\[4.0pt]
0,&amp;1\leq i&lt;j\leq L.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.4" class="ltx_p">which is exactly (<a href="#A1.E9" title="In Appendix A Proof of the Exact Cesàro Matrix Power Entry ‣ Lost in the Middle at Birth: An Exact Theory of Transformer Position Bias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) with <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> replaced by <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
By induction on <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, the formula holds for all <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq i\leq L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq i\leq L</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Integral Representation of <math id="A2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{H})_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{H})_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To simplify <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{H})_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{H})_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> we use the identity for the power function via the Gamma function:</p>
<table id="A2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{m^{H}}=\frac{1}{(H-1)!}\int_{0}^{\infty}t^{H-1}e^{-mt}dt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mi>H</mi></msup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{m^{H}}=\frac{1}{(H-1)!}\int_{0}^{\infty}t^{H-1}e^{-mt}dt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Substituting this into the summation and invoking the <span id="A2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fubini-Tonelli theorem</span> to interchange the order of summation and integration, we obtain:</p>
<table id="A2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{H})_{i,j}=\frac{1}{(H-1)!}\binom{i-1}{j-1}\int_{0}^{\infty}t^{H-1}\left[\sum_{m=j}^{i}(-1)^{m-j}\binom{i-j}{m-j}e^{-mt}\right]dt" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{H})_{i,j}=\frac{1}{(H-1)!}\binom{i-1}{j-1}\int_{0}^{\infty}t^{H-1}\left[\sum_{m=j}^{i}(-1)^{m-j}\binom{i-j}{m-j}e^{-mt}\right]dt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Letting <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=m-j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=m-j</annotation></semantics></math>, the inner sum simplifies via the Binomial Theorem:</p>
<table id="A2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{-jt}\sum_{k=0}^{i-j}\binom{i-j}{k}(-e^{-t})^{k}=e^{-jt}(1-e^{-t})^{i-j}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{-jt}\sum_{k=0}^{i-j}\binom{i-j}{k}(-e^{-t})^{k}=e^{-jt}(1-e^{-t})^{i-j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Applying the change of variables <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=e^{-t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=e^{-t}</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="dt=-du/u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dt=-du/u</annotation></semantics></math>), the integral transforms to:</p>
<table id="A2.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{H})_{i,j}=\frac{1}{(H-1)!}\binom{i-1}{j-1}\int_{0}^{1}(-\ln u)^{H-1}u^{j-1}(1-u)^{i-j}du" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{H})_{i,j}=\frac{1}{(H-1)!}\binom{i-1}{j-1}\int_{0}^{1}(-\ln u)^{H-1}u^{j-1}(1-u)^{i-j}du</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Detailed Derivations of Density Operators in the Continuum Limit</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the continuous limit <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, vectors become continuous functions <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and matrix multiplication becomes an integral operator:</p>
<table id="A3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{O}h)(y)\;=\;\int_{0}^{1}G_{1}(y,x)\,h(x)\,dx" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{O}h)(y)\;=\;\int_{0}^{1}G_{1}(y,x)\,h(x)\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}(y,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}(y,x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the <em id="A3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">integral kernel</em>. For the linear operator applied <math id="A3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> times, one obtains:</p>
<table id="A3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{O}^{n}h)(y)\;=\;\int_{0}^{1}G_{n}(y,x)\,h(x)\,dx" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{O}^{n}h)(y)\;=\;\int_{0}^{1}G_{n}(y,x)\,h(x)\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where mathematical consistency demands the convolution relation:</p>
<table id="A3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{m+n}(y,x)\;=\;\int_{0}^{1}G_{m}(y,z)\,G_{n}(z,x)\,dz" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{m+n}(y,x)\;=\;\int_{0}^{1}G_{m}(y,z)\,G_{n}(z,x)\,dz</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of autoregressive language modeling, we are specifically concerned with predicting the next token, which depends entirely on the hidden state of the final token of the prompt. We define the <em id="A3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">influence density</em> <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{n}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{n}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> as the kernel evaluated at this final token (<math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
<table id="A3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{n}(x)\;\equiv\;G_{n}(1,x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{n}(x)\;\equiv\;G_{n}(1,x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We now derive the exact closed-form influence density for both the pure causal kernel and the causal kernel with residual connections.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Influence Density for the Pure Causal Kernel</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}(y,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}(y,x)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-layer kernel for the pure causal attention operator <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. If there are no layers (<math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math>), the output is identical to the input, yielding the identity kernel:</p>
<table id="A3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}(y,x)\;=\;\delta(y-x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}(y,x)\;=\;\delta(y-x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The first non-trivial operator applies a uniform average over all past tokens:</p>
<table id="A3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{M}h)(y)\;=\;\int_{0}^{y}\frac{1}{y}\,h(x)\,dx" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>y</mi></msubsup><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>y</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{M}h)(y)\;=\;\int_{0}^{y}\frac{1}{y}\,h(x)\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">By factoring in the causal mask using the Heaviside step function <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(y-x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(y-x)</annotation></semantics></math>, we can extract the layer-1 kernel directly:</p>
<table id="A3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1}(y,x)\;=\;\frac{1}{y}\Theta(y-x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>y</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1}(y,x)\;=\;\frac{1}{y}\Theta(y-x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We will prove by mathematical induction that the general <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-layer causal kernel takes the closed form:</p>
<table id="A3.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}(y,x)\;=\;\frac{1}{y}\frac{1}{(n-1)!}\left(\ln\frac{y}{x}\right)^{n-1}\Theta(y-x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>y</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}(y,x)\;=\;\frac{1}{y}\frac{1}{(n-1)!}\left(\ln\frac{y}{x}\right)^{n-1}\Theta(y-x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Proof.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the base case <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the formula yields <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{y}\frac{1}{0!}(\ln\frac{y}{x})^{0}\Theta(y-x)=\frac{1}{y}\Theta(y-x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>y</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>y</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{y}\frac{1}{0!}(\ln\frac{y}{x})^{0}\Theta(y-x)=\frac{1}{y}\Theta(y-x)</annotation></semantics></math>, which trivially holds.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assume the formula holds for layer <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. To find the kernel for layer <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>, we apply the consistency convolution between <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A3.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n+1}(y,x)\;=\;\int_{0}^{1}K_{1}(y,z)\,K_{n}(z,x)\,dz" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n+1}(y,x)\;=\;\int_{0}^{1}K_{1}(y,z)\,K_{n}(z,x)\,dz</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Substituting our known expressions:</p>
<table id="A3.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n+1}(y,x)\;=\;\int_{0}^{1}\left[\frac{1}{y}\Theta(y-z)\right]\left[\frac{1}{z}\frac{1}{(n-1)!}\left(\ln\frac{z}{x}\right)^{n-1}\Theta(z-x)\right]dz" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>y</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>z</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>z</mi><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n+1}(y,x)\;=\;\int_{0}^{1}\left[\frac{1}{y}\Theta(y-z)\right]\left[\frac{1}{z}\frac{1}{(n-1)!}\left(\ln\frac{z}{x}\right)^{n-1}\Theta(z-x)\right]dz</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The product of the Heaviside functions <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(y-z)\Theta(z-x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(y-z)\Theta(z-x)</annotation></semantics></math> restricts the region of non-zero integration strictly to <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\leq z\leq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\leq z\leq y</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x&gt;y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&gt;y</annotation></semantics></math>, the integral vanishes, preserving the global causal mask <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(y-x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(y-x)</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\leq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\leq y</annotation></semantics></math>, we extract the constants with respect to <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A3.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n+1}(y,x)\;=\;\frac{1}{y}\frac{1}{(n-1)!}\int_{x}^{y}\frac{1}{z}\left(\ln\frac{z}{x}\right)^{n-1}dz" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>y</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi></msubsup><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>z</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>z</mi><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n+1}(y,x)\;=\;\frac{1}{y}\frac{1}{(n-1)!}\int_{x}^{y}\frac{1}{z}\left(\ln\frac{z}{x}\right)^{n-1}dz</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">We evaluate this integral using integration by substitution. Let <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\ln(z/x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\ln(z/x)</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="du=\frac{1}{z}dz" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>z</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">du=\frac{1}{z}dz</annotation></semantics></math>. The integration bounds transform from <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in[x,y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in[x,y]</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in[0,\ln(y/x)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in[0,\ln(y/x)]</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A3.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n+1}(y,x)\;=\;\frac{1}{y}\frac{1}{(n-1)!}\int_{0}^{\ln(y/x)}u^{n-1}\,du" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>y</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n+1}(y,x)\;=\;\frac{1}{y}\frac{1}{(n-1)!}\int_{0}^{\ln(y/x)}u^{n-1}\,du</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A3.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n+1}(y,x)\;=\;\frac{1}{y}\frac{1}{(n-1)!}\left[\frac{u^{n}}{n}\right]_{0}^{\ln(y/x)}\;=\;\frac{1}{y}\frac{1}{n!}\left(\ln\frac{y}{x}\right)^{n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>y</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>y</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n+1}(y,x)\;=\;\frac{1}{y}\frac{1}{(n-1)!}\left[\frac{u^{n}}{n}\right]_{0}^{\ln(y/x)}\;=\;\frac{1}{y}\frac{1}{n!}\left(\ln\frac{y}{x}\right)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Re-attaching the global causal mask <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(y-x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(y-x)</annotation></semantics></math> completes the inductive step. <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic">∎</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we evaluate this kernel at the final token <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math> to recover the macroscopic influence density of the pure causal architecture. Because the normalized sequence coordinate <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, the condition <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> is universally satisfied, meaning <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(1-x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(1-x)=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the primacy tail density after <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> layers is exactly:</p>
<table id="A3.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{n}^{(M)}(x)\;=\;K_{n}(1,x)\;=\;\frac{1}{(n-1)!}\left(\ln\frac{1}{x}\right)^{n-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{n}^{(M)}(x)\;=\;K_{n}(1,x)\;=\;\frac{1}{(n-1)!}\left(\ln\frac{1}{x}\right)^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This rigorously establishes the logarithmic divergence at the start of the prompt caused by pure causal compounding.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Influence Density for the Causal Residual Kernel</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}_{n}(y,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}_{n}(y,x)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-layer kernel for the network with both causal attention and residual connections. For a zero-layer architecture, the causal residual kernel is trivially identical to the pure causal identity kernel:</p>
<table id="A3.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}_{0}(y,x)\;=\;K_{0}(y,x)\;=\;\delta(y-x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}_{0}(y,x)\;=\;K_{0}(y,x)\;=\;\delta(y-x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The single-layer operator applies a weighted average of a residual connection and the uniform causal average over all past tokens:</p>
<table id="A3.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{N}h)(y)\;=\;(1-\alpha)h(y)+\alpha\int_{0}^{y}\frac{1}{y}h(x)\,dx" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>y</mi></msubsup><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>y</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{N}h)(y)\;=\;(1-\alpha)h(y)+\alpha\int_{0}^{y}\frac{1}{y}h(x)\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This gives us the single-layer kernel:</p>
<table id="A3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{K}_{1}(y,x)\;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{K}_{1}(y,x)\;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\;(1-\alpha)\delta(y-x)+\alpha\frac{1}{y}\Theta(y-x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>y</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\;(1-\alpha)\delta(y-x)+\alpha\frac{1}{y}\Theta(y-x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\;(1-\alpha)K_{0}(y,x)+\alpha K_{1}(y,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\;(1-\alpha)K_{0}(y,x)+\alpha K_{1}(y,x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We will prove by mathematical induction that the general <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-layer residual kernel is a binomial expansion of the pure causal kernels:</p>
<table id="A3.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}_{n}(y,x)\;=\;\sum_{k=0}^{n}\binom{n}{k}\alpha^{k}(1-\alpha)^{n-k}K_{k}(y,x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}_{n}(y,x)\;=\;\sum_{k=0}^{n}\binom{n}{k}\alpha^{k}(1-\alpha)^{n-k}K_{k}(y,x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Proof.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The base case <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> yields <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{1}{0}\alpha^{0}(1-\alpha)^{1}K_{0}+\binom{1}{1}\alpha^{1}(1-\alpha)^{0}K_{1}=(1-\alpha)K_{0}+\alpha K_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>1</mn><mn>0</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{1}{0}\alpha^{0}(1-\alpha)^{1}K_{0}+\binom{1}{1}\alpha^{1}(1-\alpha)^{0}K_{1}=(1-\alpha)K_{0}+\alpha K_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which trivially holds.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assume the formula holds for layer <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. To find the kernel for layer <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>, we apply the consistency convolution <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}_{n+1}(y,x)=\int_{0}^{1}\tilde{K}_{1}(y,z)\tilde{K}_{n}(z,x)\,dz" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}_{n+1}(y,x)=\int_{0}^{1}\tilde{K}_{1}(y,z)\tilde{K}_{n}(z,x)\,dz</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A3.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}_{n+1}\;=\;\int_{0}^{1}\Big[(1-\alpha)K_{0}(y,z)+\alpha K_{1}(y,z)\Big]\left[\sum_{k=0}^{n}\binom{n}{k}\alpha^{k}(1-\alpha)^{n-k}K_{k}(z,x)\right]dz" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}_{n+1}\;=\;\int_{0}^{1}\Big[(1-\alpha)K_{0}(y,z)+\alpha K_{1}(y,z)\Big]\left[\sum_{k=0}^{n}\binom{n}{k}\alpha^{k}(1-\alpha)^{n-k}K_{k}(z,x)\right]dz</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Because <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}(y,z)=\delta(y-z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}(y,z)=\delta(y-z)</annotation></semantics></math>, its convolution simply extracts the <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-layer kernel. For <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we rely on the consistency relation proved in the previous section: <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{0}^{1}K_{1}(y,z)K_{k}(z,x)dz=K_{k+1}(y,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{0}^{1}K_{1}(y,z)K_{k}(z,x)dz=K_{k+1}(y,x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Distributing the integral gives:</p>
<table id="A3.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}_{n+1}\;=\;(1-\alpha)\sum_{k=0}^{n}\binom{n}{k}\alpha^{k}(1-\alpha)^{n-k}K_{k}+\alpha\sum_{k=0}^{n}\binom{n}{k}\alpha^{k}(1-\alpha)^{n-k}K_{k+1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}_{n+1}\;=\;(1-\alpha)\sum_{k=0}^{n}\binom{n}{k}\alpha^{k}(1-\alpha)^{n-k}K_{k}+\alpha\sum_{k=0}^{n}\binom{n}{k}\alpha^{k}(1-\alpha)^{n-k}K_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">To combine these sums, we re-index the second sum by letting <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=k+1</annotation></semantics></math>, and we substitute <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=k</annotation></semantics></math> in the first sum:</p>
<table id="A3.E35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}_{n+1}\;=\;\sum_{j=0}^{n}\binom{n}{j}\alpha^{j}(1-\alpha)^{n+1-j}K_{j}+\sum_{j=1}^{n+1}\binom{n}{j-1}\alpha^{j}(1-\alpha)^{n+1-j}K_{j}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}_{n+1}\;=\;\sum_{j=0}^{n}\binom{n}{j}\alpha^{j}(1-\alpha)^{n+1-j}K_{j}+\sum_{j=1}^{n+1}\binom{n}{j-1}\alpha^{j}(1-\alpha)^{n+1-j}K_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">We group the terms by <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=n+1</annotation></semantics></math>, the boundary terms map perfectly to <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n+1}{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>0</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n+1}{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n+1}{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n+1}{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>, we factor out the common coefficients:</p>
<table id="A3.E36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left[\binom{n}{j}+\binom{n}{j-1}\right]\alpha^{j}(1-\alpha)^{n+1-j}K_{j}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left[\binom{n}{j}+\binom{n}{j-1}\right]\alpha^{j}(1-\alpha)^{n+1-j}K_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">By Pascal’s Rule, <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n}{j}+\binom{n}{j-1}=\binom{n+1}{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n}{j}+\binom{n}{j-1}=\binom{n+1}{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting this identity confirms that the combined sum is exactly:</p>
<table id="A3.E37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{K}_{n+1}(y,x)\;=\;\sum_{j=0}^{n+1}\binom{n+1}{j}\alpha^{j}(1-\alpha)^{n+1-j}K_{j}(y,x)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{K}_{n+1}(y,x)\;=\;\sum_{j=0}^{n+1}\binom{n+1}{j}\alpha^{j}(1-\alpha)^{n+1-j}K_{j}(y,x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.6" class="ltx_p">This completes the inductive step. <span id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic">∎</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To find the final influence density for the residual network, we evaluate this full kernel at <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Separating the purely residual <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math> term from the causal paths <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and substituting our closed-form <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{k}(1,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{k}(1,x)</annotation></semantics></math> expressions, we obtain the exact architectural baseline:</p>
<table id="A3.E38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{n}^{(\mathcal{N})}(x)\;=\;\underbrace{(1-\alpha)^{n}\delta(1-x)}_{\text{Recency Anchor}}\;+\;\underbrace{\sum_{k=1}^{n}\binom{n}{k}\alpha^{k}(1-\alpha)^{n-k}\frac{1}{(k-1)!}\left(\ln\frac{1}{x}\right)^{k-1}}_{\text{Continuous Causal \&amp; Hybrid Paths}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Recency Anchor</mtext></munder><mo rspace="0.335em">+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Continuous Causal &amp; Hybrid Paths</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{n}^{(\mathcal{N})}(x)\;=\;\underbrace{(1-\alpha)^{n}\delta(1-x)}_{\text{Recency Anchor}}\;+\;\underbrace{\sum_{k=1}^{n}\binom{n}{k}\alpha^{k}(1-\alpha)^{n-k}\frac{1}{(k-1)!}\left(\ln\frac{1}{x}\right)^{k-1}}_{\text{Continuous Causal \&amp; Hybrid Paths}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This derivation proves that causal mixing inherently creates the logarithmic primacy tail, but the residual stream bypasses the mixing matrix entirely (<math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math>), resulting in an infinite Dirac density spike exactly at <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>From Full Transformer Layer to the Cesàro Toy Model</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix makes precise which components of a full transformer
layer are retained in the positional routing model of
Section <a href="#S3" title="3 The Exact Calculus of Position Bias ‣ Lost in the Middle at Birth: An Exact Theory of Transformer Position Bias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, following the linearisation philosophy of
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Elhage et al. [8]</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Full Layer</h3>

<div id="A4.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A single pre-norm transformer block maps <math id="A4.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(0)}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(0)}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A4.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> via:</p>
<table id="A4.E39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}_{i}=h^{(0)}_{i}+\tilde{\alpha}\,(M\,g(h^{(0)}))_{i}+f\!\Big(g\!\big(h^{(0)}_{i}+\tilde{\alpha}\,(M\,g(h^{(0)}))_{i}\big)\Big)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.427em;" width="0.427em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.343em;" width="0.343em"><mi>g</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}_{i}=h^{(0)}_{i}+\tilde{\alpha}\,(M\,g(h^{(0)}))_{i}+f\!\Big(g\!\big(h^{(0)}_{i}+\tilde{\alpha}\,(M\,g(h^{(0)}))_{i}\big)\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SSx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.SSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{ij}=\frac{1}{i}\,\Theta(i-j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{ij}=\frac{1}{i}\,\Theta(i-j)</annotation></semantics></math> is the causal averaging
(Cesàro) matrix, <math id="A4.SSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=\mathrm{RMSNorm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>RMSNorm</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=\mathrm{RMSNorm}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A4.SSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=\mathrm{GELU}(x\,W_{1})\,W_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>GELU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=\mathrm{GELU}(x\,W_{1})\,W_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the feed-forward network,
and <math id="A4.SSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\alpha}=\|W_{V}W_{O}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\alpha}=\|W_{V}W_{O}\|</annotation></semantics></math> is the gain of the value-output
pathway (denoted <math id="A4.SSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Full Single-Head Jacobian and Reduction to the Cesàro Kernel ‣ Lost in the Middle at Birth: An Exact Theory of Transformer Position Bias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>).
Only <math id="A4.SSx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> mixes information across token positions; <math id="A4.SSx1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SSx1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>
act per-token.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Simplifications</h3>

<div id="A4.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We make two simplifications to isolate the positional routing:</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A4.E40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E40.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="h^{(1)}_{i}=\underbrace{h^{(0)}_{i}\vphantom{\Big(}}_{\text{keep}}+\underbrace{\tilde{\alpha}\vphantom{\Big(}}_{\text{keep}}\underbrace{(\,M\vphantom{\Big(}}_{\text{keep}}\underbrace{g(h^{(0)})\,)_{i}}_{\begin{subarray}{c}\text{drop }g:\\
g\to\mathrm{Id}\end{subarray}}+\underbrace{f\!\Big(g\!\big(h^{(0)}_{i}+\tilde{\alpha}(Mg(h^{(0)}))_{i}\big)\Big)}_{\text{drop entirely}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><munder accentunder="true"><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>keep</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><munderover accent="true" accentunder="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo><mo>~</mo></munderover><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>keep</mtext></msub><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>keep</mtext></msub><munder accentunder="true"><msub><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mtext>drop </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Id</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mo>+</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.427em;" width="0.427em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.343em;" width="0.343em"><mi>g</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>drop entirely</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}_{i}=\underbrace{h^{(0)}_{i}\vphantom{\Big(}}_{\text{keep}}+\underbrace{\tilde{\alpha}\vphantom{\Big(}}_{\text{keep}}\underbrace{(\,M\vphantom{\Big(}}_{\text{keep}}\underbrace{g(h^{(0)})\,)_{i}}_{\begin{subarray}{c}\text{drop }g:\\
g\to\mathrm{Id}\end{subarray}}+\underbrace{f\!\Big(g\!\big(h^{(0)}_{i}+\tilde{\alpha}(Mg(h^{(0)}))_{i}\big)\Big)}_{\text{drop entirely}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A4.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="A4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Drop <math id="A4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> (RMSNorm).</span>
At initialisation, RMSNorm rescales all tokens to the same RMS,
contributing a position-independent per-token gain. Removing it
preserves the positional routing structure.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Drop <math id="A4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> (FFN).</span>
The feed-forward network acts per-token and does not directly
introduce cross-position mixing. However, its input
<math id="A4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(0.5)}_{i}=h^{(0)}_{i}+\tilde{\alpha}(Mg(h^{(0)}))_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(0.5)}_{i}=h^{(0)}_{i}+\tilde{\alpha}(Mg(h^{(0)}))_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>
already contains the attention output, so the nonlinearity in
<math id="A4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> couples to the positional mixing from <math id="A4.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
Section <a href="#S3" title="3 The Exact Calculus of Position Bias ‣ Lost in the Middle at Birth: An Exact Theory of Transformer Position Bias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> notes that the FFN Jacobian is
block-diagonal and does not alter the topology of horizontal
routing; dropping <math id="A4.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> removes the nonlinear coupling while
preserving this property.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="A4.SSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The resulting toy model is:</p>
<table id="A4.E41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}=(I+\tilde{\alpha}\,M)\,h^{(0)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}=(I+\tilde{\alpha}\,M)\,h^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(41)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A4.SSx2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SSx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">After <math id="A4.SSx2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> layers:</p>
<table id="A4.E42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(H)}=(I+\tilde{\alpha}\,M)^{H}\,h^{(0)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>H</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(H)}=(I+\tilde{\alpha}\,M)^{H}\,h^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(42)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Normalised Form</h3>

<div id="A4.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rows of <math id="A4.SSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I+\tilde{\alpha}\,M)^{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>H</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I+\tilde{\alpha}\,M)^{H}</annotation></semantics></math> sum to
<math id="A4.SSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+\tilde{\alpha})^{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>H</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+\tilde{\alpha})^{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, reflecting the fact that the residual
connection adds signal rather than redistributing it. For the
purpose of studying the <em id="A4.SSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">shape</em> of the influence profile, we
normalise by defining</p>
<table id="A4.E43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\frac{\tilde{\alpha}}{1+\tilde{\alpha}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\frac{\tilde{\alpha}}{1+\tilde{\alpha}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(43)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SSx3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">so that</p>
<table id="A4.E44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=(1-\alpha)\,I+\alpha\,M" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=(1-\alpha)\,I+\alpha\,M</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(44)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SSx3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">is a stochastic matrix whose rows sum to one, and <math id="A4.SSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N^{H})_{L,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N^{H})_{L,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is
a probability distribution over source positions. This is the form
used throughout the paper. The physical gain
<math id="A4.SSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\alpha}=\gamma=\|W_{V}W_{O}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\alpha}=\gamma=\|W_{V}W_{O}\|</annotation></semantics></math> relates to the fitted
parameter via <math id="A4.SSx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\alpha}=\alpha/(1-\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\alpha}=\alpha/(1-\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that <math id="A4.SSx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{H}</annotation></semantics></math> factors out as a position-uniform constant
(Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Full Single-Head Jacobian and Reduction to the Cesàro Kernel ‣ Lost in the Middle at Birth: An Exact Theory of Transformer Position Bias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>), so the shape of the U-curve
depends only on <math id="A4.SSx3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, not on <math id="A4.SSx3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> separately.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Full Single-Head Jacobian and Reduction to the Cesàro Kernel</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix derives the complete back-propagation Jacobian of a single
causal attention layer, identifies precisely the roles of <math id="A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{O}</annotation></semantics></math>, and shows the exact approximation step that reduces the
position-routing component to the scalar Cesàro kernel used throughout
the paper.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Setup</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a single attention head with input sequence
<math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}^{(l)}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}^{(l)}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The standard attention mechanism computes:</p>
<table id="A5.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.E45"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbf{H}^{(l)}W_{Q},\quad K=\mathbf{H}^{(l)}W_{K},\quad V=\mathbf{H}^{(l)}W_{V}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbf{H}^{(l)}W_{Q},\quad K=\mathbf{H}^{(l)}W_{K},\quad V=\mathbf{H}^{(l)}W_{V},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(45)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.E46"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{q_{i}\cdot k_{j}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}+m_{ij},\qquad m_{ij}=-\infty\text{ for }j&gt;i," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{q_{i}\cdot k_{j}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}+m_{ij},\qquad m_{ij}=-\infty\text{ for }j&gt;i,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(46)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.E47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{i,\cdot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{i,\cdot}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E47.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathrm{softmax}(S_{i,\cdot})\in\mathbb{R}^{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathrm{softmax}(S_{i,\cdot})\in\mathbb{R}^{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(47)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A5.E48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{j\leq i}A_{ij}\,v_{j},\qquad\mathbf{o}_{i}=\mathbf{z}_{i}W_{O}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{j\leq i}A_{ij}\,v_{j},\qquad\mathbf{o}_{i}=\mathbf{z}_{i}W_{O}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(48)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">With a residual connection, the layer update is
<math id="A5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(l+1)}_{i}=\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_{i}+\mathbf{o}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐨</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(l+1)}_{i}=\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_{i}+\mathbf{o}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Full Per-Layer Jacobian</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Jacobian of the <em id="A5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">output</em> at position <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the
<em id="A5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">input</em> at position <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leq i</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="A5.E49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\mathbf{h}^{(l+1)}_{i}}{\partial\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_{j}}\;=\;\delta_{ij}\,I_{d}\;+\;\underbrace{A_{ij}\,W_{V}W_{O}}_{\displaystyle\text{Value Pathway}}\;+\;\underbrace{\left(\sum_{k\leq i}\frac{\partial A_{ik}}{\partial\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_{j}}\,v_{k}\right)W_{O}}_{\displaystyle\text{Score Pathway}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Value Pathway</mtext></munder><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Score Pathway</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\mathbf{h}^{(l+1)}_{i}}{\partial\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_{j}}\;=\;\delta_{ij}\,I_{d}\;+\;\underbrace{A_{ij}\,W_{V}W_{O}}_{\displaystyle\text{Value Pathway}}\;+\;\underbrace{\left(\sum_{k\leq i}\frac{\partial A_{ik}}{\partial\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_{j}}\,v_{k}\right)W_{O}}_{\displaystyle\text{Score Pathway}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(49)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Value Pathway.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Perturbing <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> changes <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{j}=\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_{j}W_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{j}=\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_{j}W_{V}</annotation></semantics></math>
directly. This perturbation is mixed into <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with weight
<math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, then projected by <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{O}</annotation></semantics></math>. This pathway is active for all
<math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leq i</annotation></semantics></math> and is the direct, “vertical” route through <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{V}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Score Pathway.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Perturbing <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> also changes <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, modifying
the attention scores <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence the routing weights <math id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using the chain rule through the causal Softmax:</p>
<table id="A5.E50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial A_{ik}}{\partial\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_{j}}\;=\;\sum_{m\leq i}A_{ik}\bigl(\delta_{km}-A_{im}\bigr)\left[\delta_{ij}\,\frac{W_{Q}\,k_{m}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}\;+\;\delta_{mj}\,\frac{q_{i}\,W_{K}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial A_{ik}}{\partial\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_{j}}\;=\;\sum_{m\leq i}A_{ik}\bigl(\delta_{km}-A_{im}\bigr)\left[\delta_{ij}\,\frac{W_{Q}\,k_{m}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}\;+\;\delta_{mj}\,\frac{q_{i}\,W_{K}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(50)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The two Kronecker deltas correspond to two distinct mechanisms:</p>
<ul id="A5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>: the query <math id="A5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> changes (only when <math id="A5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=i</annotation></semantics></math>,
i.e. a token attending to itself).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{mj}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{mj}</annotation></semantics></math>: the key <math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> changes, modifying how much every
later token attends to position <math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.3 </span>Why the Score Pathway Vanishes at initialization</h3>

<div id="A5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At random Gaussian initialization, <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> are zero-mean with
entries <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{O}(d_{k}^{-1/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(d_{k}^{-1/2})</annotation></semantics></math> (standard Kaiming or Xavier scaling). Consequently:</p>
<ol id="A5.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The pre-softmax scores satisfy
<math id="A5.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{ij}=q_{i}\cdot k_{j}/\sqrt{d_{k}}\;\xrightarrow{d_{k}\to\infty}\;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0.558em">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">k</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mover><mn> 0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{ij}=q_{i}\cdot k_{j}/\sqrt{d_{k}}\;\xrightarrow{d_{k}\to\infty}\;0</annotation></semantics></math>,
and so the causal Softmax is approximately uniform:
<math id="A5.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ij}\approx 1/i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ij}\approx 1/i</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A5.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leq i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Softmax Jacobian at a uniform distribution evaluates to
<math id="A5.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ik}(\delta_{km}-A_{im})=(1/i)(\delta_{km}-1/i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ik}(\delta_{km}-A_{im})=(1/i)(\delta_{km}-1/i)</annotation></semantics></math>,
which is <math id="A5.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/i)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A5.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each term in the Score Pathway (Eq. <a href="#A5.E50" title="In Score Pathway. ‣ E.2 Full Per-Layer Jacobian ‣ Appendix E Full Single-Head Jacobian and Reduction to the Cesàro Kernel ‣ Lost in the Middle at Birth: An Exact Theory of Transformer Position Bias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>) carries
an additional factor of <math id="A5.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{Q}k_{m}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{Q}k_{m}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A5.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}W_{K}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}W_{K}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, both
<math id="A5.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{O}(d_{k}^{-1/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(d_{k}^{-1/2})</annotation></semantics></math>, which suppresses the Score Pathway by
<math id="A5.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{O}(d_{k}^{-1/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(d_{k}^{-1/2})</annotation></semantics></math> relative to the Value Pathway.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the standard wide-network regime (<math id="A5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k}\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k}\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>), the Score Pathway
contribution vanishes at initialization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. This is the formal justification for the Softmax
linearisation used in the main text.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.4 </span>Reduction to the Scalar Cesàro Kernel</h3>

<div id="A5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Retaining only the Value Pathway, Eq. <a href="#A5.E49" title="In E.2 Full Per-Layer Jacobian ‣ Appendix E Full Single-Head Jacobian and Reduction to the Cesàro Kernel ‣ Lost in the Middle at Birth: An Exact Theory of Transformer Position Bias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a> simplifies to:</p>
<table id="A5.E51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\mathbf{h}^{(l+1)}_{i}}{\partial\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_{j}}\;\approx\;\delta_{ij}\,I_{d}\;+\;A_{ij}\,W_{V}W_{O}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\mathbf{h}^{(l+1)}_{i}}{\partial\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_{j}}\;\approx\;\delta_{ij}\,I_{d}\;+\;A_{ij}\,W_{V}W_{O}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(51)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The positional structure is governed entirely by the scalar <math id="A5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, while
<math id="A5.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{V}W_{O}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{V}W_{O}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a position-<em id="A5.SS4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">invariant</em> feature
mixing matrix, identical for all token pairs <math id="A5.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The end-to-end Jacobian after <math id="A5.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> layers is obtained by chaining these
terms. Taking norms and factoring out the position-independent “vertical”
gain <math id="A5.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\|W_{V}W_{O}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\|W_{V}W_{O}\|</annotation></semantics></math> per layer:</p>
<table id="A5.E52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(j)\;=\;\left\|\frac{\partial\mathbf{h}^{(H)}_{L}}{\partial\mathbf{h}^{(0)}_{j}}\right\|\;\approx\;\gamma^{H}\cdot(N^{H})_{L,j}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.280em">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(j)\;=\;\left\|\frac{\partial\mathbf{h}^{(H)}_{L}}{\partial\mathbf{h}^{(0)}_{j}}\right\|\;\approx\;\gamma^{H}\cdot(N^{H})_{L,j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(52)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=(1-\alpha)I+\alpha M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=(1-\alpha)I+\alpha M</annotation></semantics></math> is the residual Cesàro matrix and
<math id="A5.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N^{H})_{L,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N^{H})_{L,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is its <math id="A5.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L,j)</annotation></semantics></math> entry. Since <math id="A5.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant that
shifts all positions uniformly on the log scale, it has no effect on the
<em id="A5.SS4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">shape</em> of the U-curve.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Conclusion.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> does not generate the kernel: it provides a uniform scalar gain that
is identical at every position. The asymmetric U-shape is an exclusive
property of the scalar routing matrix <math id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{H}</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{H}</annotation></semantics></math>), determined solely
by the causal mask and the residual mixing parameter <math id="A5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.5 </span>Multi-Head Attention at Initialization</h3>

<div id="A5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At initialization, all heads share the same uniform attention
distribution <math id="A5.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ij}=1/i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ij}=1/i</annotation></semantics></math>, so the expected positional structure is
identical to the single-head Cesàro kernel. However, each head
contributes an independent random gain <math id="A5.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{V}^{h}W_{O}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi><mi>h</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{V}^{h}W_{O}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the total
Jacobian is a sum of <math id="A5.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\text{heads}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mtext>heads</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\text{heads}}</annotation></semantics></math> independent terms with
identical positional structure. By concentration of measure, the
empirical Jacobian norm converges to the theoretical prediction as
<math id="A5.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{H_{\text{heads}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>H</mi><mtext>heads</mtext></msub></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{H_{\text{heads}}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Increasing the number of
heads therefore <em id="A5.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tightens</em> the architectural prior: the U-shape
becomes less variable across random initializations, not more.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>What is Plotted: The Scalar Surrogate for the Jacobian Norm</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We measure how strongly each input position <math id="A6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> influences the model’s <em id="A6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">final</em> prediction at the last position <math id="A6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. Let the input embedding matrix be <math id="A6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{j}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{j}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> representing the embedding vector at position <math id="A6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. Let the final-layer hidden state at the last position be <math id="A6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{L}^{(H)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{L}^{(H)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the logit vector (pre-softmax) at the last position as:</p>
<table id="A6.E53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\;=\;W_{U}h_{L}^{(H)}\in\mathbb{R}^{|V|}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\;=\;W_{U}h_{L}^{(H)}\in\mathbb{R}^{|V|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(53)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{|V|\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{|V|\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unembedding matrix.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The full input-output Jacobian of the last-position logits with respect to the input embeddings is the tensor:</p>
<table id="A6.E54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\;=\;\frac{\partial\ell}{\partial E}\in\mathbb{R}^{|V|\times L\times d}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\;=\;\frac{\partial\ell}{\partial E}\in\mathbb{R}^{|V|\times L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(54)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We define the per-position slice of this Jacobian as <math id="A6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{j}\equiv\frac{\partial\ell}{\partial e_{j}}\in\mathbb{R}^{|V|\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{j}\equiv\frac{\partial\ell}{\partial e_{j}}\in\mathbb{R}^{|V|\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A mathematically natural scalar “influence score” per position would be the true Frobenius norm of this slice:</p>
<table id="A6.E55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{F}(j)\;=\;\left\|J_{j}\right\|_{F}\;=\;\sqrt{\sum_{v=1}^{|V|}\sum_{k=1}^{d}\left(\frac{\partial\ell_{v}}{\partial e_{j,k}}\right)^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msqrt><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{F}(j)\;=\;\left\|J_{j}\right\|_{F}\;=\;\sqrt{\sum_{v=1}^{|V|}\sum_{k=1}^{d}\left(\frac{\partial\ell_{v}}{\partial e_{j,k}}\right)^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(55)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p3.2" class="ltx_p">However, computing <math id="A6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|J_{j}\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|J_{j}\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> exactly is computationally expensive, as it requires aggregating gradient information from all <math id="A6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V|</annotation></semantics></math> independent logits (effectively requiring <math id="A6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V|</annotation></semantics></math> separate backward passes).</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Instead, our experimental code utilizes a highly efficient one-pass <em id="A6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">scalar-probe</em> surrogate. We choose a fixed probe vector <math id="A6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathbb{R}^{|V|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathbb{R}^{|V|}</annotation></semantics></math> (in our implementation, the all-ones vector <math id="A6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math>), and define the scalar surrogate:</p>
<table id="A6.E56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\;=\;u^{\top}\ell=\sum_{v=1}^{|V|}\ell_{v}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\;=\;u^{\top}\ell=\sum_{v=1}^{|V|}\ell_{v}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(56)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p4.2" class="ltx_p">A single backward pass from this scalar <math id="A6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> yields the <math id="A6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional gradient vector:</p>
<table id="A6.E57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{j}\;\equiv\;\frac{\partial s}{\partial e_{j}}\;=\;\left(\frac{\partial\ell}{\partial e_{j}}\right)^{\top}u\;=\;J_{j}^{\top}u\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{j}\;\equiv\;\frac{\partial s}{\partial e_{j}}\;=\;\left(\frac{\partial\ell}{\partial e_{j}}\right)^{\top}u\;=\;J_{j}^{\top}u\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(57)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The quantity we plot is the standard <math id="A6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (Euclidean) norm of this vector:</p>
<table id="A6.E58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(j)\;\equiv\;\|g_{j}\|_{2}\;=\;\sqrt{\sum_{k=1}^{d}(g_{j,k})^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msqrt><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(j)\;\equiv\;\|g_{j}\|_{2}\;=\;\sqrt{\sum_{k=1}^{d}(g_{j,k})^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(58)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Importantly, <math id="A6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(j)=\|J_{j}^{\top}u\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(j)=\|J_{j}^{\top}u\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="A6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> identically equal to the Frobenius norm <math id="A6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|J_{j}\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|J_{j}\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking the Euclidean norm of a sum of vectors is not equivalent to the square root of the sum of their squared norms.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">However, in the structural regime studied in this paper, this surrogate perfectly preserves the macroscopic topology. The position-dependent structure of the network is dominated exclusively by the scalar routing kernel <math id="A6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N^{H})_{L,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N^{H})_{L,j}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the remaining feature mixing (including the accumulated products of <math id="A6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{V}W_{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{V}W_{O}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>) behaves as a position-invariant matrix <math id="A6.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in\mathbb{R}^{|V|\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in\mathbb{R}^{|V|\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Because we can factor <math id="A6.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{j}\approx(N^{H})_{L,j}\cdot C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{j}\approx(N^{H})_{L,j}\cdot C</annotation></semantics></math>, both the Frobenius norm and our scalar surrogate scale linearly with the structural kernel:</p>
<table id="A6.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A6.E59"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\rho_{F}(j)\;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\rho_{F}(j)\;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E59.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\;|(N^{H})_{L,j}|\cdot\|C\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\;|(N^{H})_{L,j}|\cdot\|C\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(59)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A6.E60"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\rho(j)\;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\rho(j)\;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E60.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\;|(N^{H})_{L,j}|\cdot\|C^{\top}u\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.558em">∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\;|(N^{H})_{L,j}|\cdot\|C^{\top}u\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(60)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Because the <math id="A6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm behaves dimensionally identical to the square root of a sum of squares (analogous to RMSE, not MSE), taking the logarithm separates the structural kernel from the position-invariant constants cleanly. Concretely, the quantity displayed in our figures is plotted on a logarithmic scale as:</p>
<table id="A6.E61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.E61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{10}\rho(j)\;=\;H\log_{10}\gamma\;+\;\log_{10}(N^{H})_{L,j}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{10}\rho(j)\;=\;H\log_{10}\gamma\;+\;\log_{10}(N^{H})_{L,j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(61)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> collects the position-invariant constants from the feature-mixing matrices. Thus, the positional <em id="A6.p7.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">shape</em> of the empirical U-curve is governed exclusively by <math id="A6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N^{H})_{L,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N^{H})_{L,j}</annotation></semantics></math>, confirming that our scalar surrogate rigorously captures the exact geometric topology of the network.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Training Evolution Experiment: Setup and Methodology</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To observe how the architectural prior evolves during early training, we
conduct a controlled micro-training experiment on a freshly initialised
Qwen2-0.5B architecture (494M parameters, 24 layers, 14Q/2KV GQA,
<math id="A7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=896" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>896</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=896</annotation></semantics></math>, SwiGLU, RMSNorm, <math id="A7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{rope}}=10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>rope</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{rope}}=10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math>, sequence length
<math id="A7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=2048" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=2048</annotation></semantics></math>). No pretrained weights are loaded.</p>
</div>
<section id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data.</h4>

<div id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We stream the NaturalQuestions (NQ) validation set and buffer the first
60 examples. Examples 0–49 form the training pool; examples 50–59 are
held out for evaluation and are never seen during training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training.</h4>

<div id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The model is trained for 100 steps using AdamW
(<math id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{lr}=5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>lr</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{lr}=5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> decayed to <math id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> via cosine
schedule, weight decay <math id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, gradient clipping at <math id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size 1).
At each step, a random contiguous window of 2048 tokens is drawn from a
randomly selected training-pool sequence and a standard next-token
cross-entropy loss is computed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Eval sequences.</h4>

<div id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct two parallel evaluation sets from the 10 held-out examples:</p>
<ul id="A7.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vanilla.</span> Each held-out NQ example is tokenised as a
single document (question concatenated with document tokens),
truncated to 2048 tokens.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Chunked.</span> Each eval sequence is constructed by
concatenating 300-token excerpts drawn from different
<em id="A7.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">training-pool</em> documents. A per-sequence offset cycles through
the pool so that content varies across sequences, but chunk
boundaries are aligned at fixed token positions
(0, 300, 600, …) across all 10 sequences. No separator or
delimiter tokens are inserted between chunks. This alignment ensures
that any boundary effects are preserved—rather than washed
out—when averaging Jacobians across sequences.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Jacobian measurement.</h4>

<div id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before each gradient update (and once after the final step), we compute
the <math id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm of the input-embedding Jacobian (as defined in
Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F What is Plotted: The Scalar Surrogate for the Jacobian Norm ‣ Lost in the Middle at Birth: An Exact Theory of Transformer Position Bias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>) for all 10 vanilla and 10 chunked eval
sequences independently. This produces two arrays of shape
<math id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(10,101,2048)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>10,101</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2048</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(10,101,2048)</annotation></semantics></math>, recording the full evolution of the Jacobian norm
profile from Step 0 through Step 100.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A8" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix H </span>Generality of the Phenomenon Across Architectures</h2>

<div id="A8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate that the asymmetric U-shape is a universal geometric property invariant to depth or specific architectural choices (e.g., RoPE, SwiGLU, RMSNorm), we provide the corresponding initialization and pretraining Jacobian comparisons for the classic GPT-2 series (GPT-2 Small, <math id="A8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=12</annotation></semantics></math>; GPT-2 Medium, <math id="A8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=24</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figures <a href="#A8.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix H Generality of the Phenomenon Across Architectures ‣ Lost in the Middle at Birth: An Exact Theory of Transformer Position Bias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, <a href="#A8.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix H Generality of the Phenomenon Across Architectures ‣ Lost in the Middle at Birth: An Exact Theory of Transformer Position Bias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, <a href="#A8.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Appendix H Generality of the Phenomenon Across Architectures ‣ Lost in the Middle at Birth: An Exact Theory of Transformer Position Bias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and <a href="#A8.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Appendix H Generality of the Phenomenon Across Architectures ‣ Lost in the Middle at Birth: An Exact Theory of Transformer Position Bias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, models utilizing standard LayerNorm, absolute positional encodings, and Gelu activations exhibit identical baseline topologies to the modern Qwen2 architecture shown in Figures <a href="#S5.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 5 Experimental Validation on Deep Architectures ‣ Lost in the Middle at Birth: An Exact Theory of Transformer Position Bias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S6.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 6 Initialization vs. Pretraining: The Rigid Topological Baseline ‣ Lost in the Middle at Birth: An Exact Theory of Transformer Position Bias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. In all cases, the topological U-shape dominates at initialization, and its macroscopic constraints persist rigidly through pretraining.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A8.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
